Iranian courts have ordered the United States to pay $290 billion in damages for decades of US actions against the Islamic Republic, a judiciary official said Monday. He added that lawsuits have also been filed against Gulf Arab states accused of letting Washington use their territory to strike Iran.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d7a75eea-1b1f-11f1-86b4-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26067484115759.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d7a75eea-1b1f-11f1-86b4-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26067484115759.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c1631b3e-e751-11f1-941c-6b60b041de3b</guid><pubDate>Mon, 23 Nov 2026 15:50:11 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d7a75eea-1b1f-11f1-86b4-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26067484115759.jpg">© Alireza Sotakbar, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>Dhaka bursting at the seams: Bangladeshi capital faces public health, housing crisis</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261123-dhaka-bursting-at-the-seams-bangladeshi-capital-faces-public-health-housing-crisis</link><description>
The Bangladeshi capital Dhaka is one of the most densely populated places on Earth, with over 35,000 people per square kilometre, compared with 20,000 in Paris. Every year, 400,000 people from across Bangladesh move to the city. Its rapid and unplanned growth is triggering a crisis in housing, transport and public health. FRANCE 24's Gabrielle Gohin and Navodita Kumari report.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cbfbc936-e73f-11f1-9fd5-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5026a01d.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cbfbc936-e73f-11f1-9fd5-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5026a01d.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f0d833d4-e4fb-11f1-8e1a-198467a7506d</guid><pubDate>Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:39:25 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Gabrielle Gohin</dc:creator></item><item><category>Asia / Pacific</category><title>Thousands rally in Istanbul against Turkey’s crackdown on opposition and media</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20261123-istanbul-turkey-crackdown-opposition-media-turkey</link><description>
Thousands marched in Istanbul on Sunday against a widening crackdown on Turkey’s opposition and independent media, days after police raided Sozcu TV and arrested senior staff. The main opposition Yeni Parti organised the protest after authorities installed a government-appointed trustee at the broadcaster, following similar moves against other independent outlets.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/40108f12-e6a7-11f1-bedd-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/2411dd227c16647a04f865f82077a651e2486d41.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/40108f12-e6a7-11f1-bedd-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/2411dd227c16647a04f865f82077a651e2486d41.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4800eae6-e6e3-11f1-8dfe-2fb0ef8e8037</guid><pubDate>Mon, 23 Nov 2026 02:15:34 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/40108f12-e6a7-11f1-bedd-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/2411dd227c16647a04f865f82077a651e2486d41.jpg">© BERK OZKAN, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Asia / Pacific</category><title>The week in pictures: Xi hosts Trump and Putin, COP31 and new French protests</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20261122-the-week-in-pictures-xi-hosts-trump-and-putin-cop31-and-more-french-protests</link><description>
From the unprecedented Shenzhen summit in China that saw President Xi Jinping host his US and Russian counterparts, Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin, to tough talks at COP31 in Antalya, Turkey and another week of French protests over the cost of living, FRANCE 24 looks back at some of the most striking images from the past week.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4d97c630-e4c9-11f1-8b5e-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-20T080754Z-1839523102-RC2IROA511RL-RTRMADP-3-CLIMATE-COP31.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4d97c630-e4c9-11f1-8b5e-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-20T080754Z-1839523102-RC2IROA511RL-RTRMADP-3-CLIMATE-COP31.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">87ebbd6e-e677-11f1-a837-3fcfcd6ec6e9</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:22:56 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4d97c630-e4c9-11f1-8b5e-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-20T080754Z-1839523102-RC2IROA511RL-RTRMADP-3-CLIMATE-COP31.jpg">© Kaan Soyturk, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Indonesia factory hit by deadly blaze: Three killed and 36 missing</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/indonesia-factory-hit-by-deadly-blaze-three-killed-and-36-missing</link><description>
At least three people have been killed and dozens are missing after a fire broke out at a factory in Indonesia producing sweets and snack products. Fifteen people were rescued alive from the plant, while firefighters were still battling flames in several parts of the facility more than 12 hours after the blaze began. The cause of the fire remains unknown, but survivors said it started in a cooling unit on the third floor.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f94de65e-e66c-11f1-93f4-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261122-120836-121007-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f94de65e-e66c-11f1-93f4-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261122-120836-121007-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fe92e70e-e66c-11f1-9cbd-af2c2c013b6d</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:11:00 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Asia / Pacific</category><title>South Korean education minister resigns over AI-driven exam cheating scandal</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20261122-south-korea-education-chief-resigns-amid-ai-cheating-scandal</link><description>
South Korea Education Minister Choi Kyo-jin resigned Sunday over an AI-assisted cheating scandal that erupted days before the high-stakes Suneung university entrance exam. The Suneung concluded "without any major incidents" last week, Choi said, but he admitted to a failure to "prepare adequately in advance".  
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/43d4fdb6-e627-11f1-b6c0-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/15c35b71c836cc0a45a17a8bf1b03197d906546e.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/43d4fdb6-e627-11f1-b6c0-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/15c35b71c836cc0a45a17a8bf1b03197d906546e.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">44964d86-e627-11f1-9ea8-fbd4eedfc338</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 03:46:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/43d4fdb6-e627-11f1-b6c0-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/15c35b71c836cc0a45a17a8bf1b03197d906546e.jpg">© Jung Yeon-je, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>EU-Indonesia agreement sees the light after ten years of negotiations</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261121-eu-indonesia-agreement-sees-the-light-after-ten-years-of-negotiations</link><description>
The European Union and Indonesia have signed a major trade deal, ten years after talks began.The agreement removes almost all tariffs between the EU and Southeast Asia's biggest economy: 98.5 per cent of them. For Europe, it means access to a market of 280 million people and a steadier supply of nickel for batteries. For Indonesia, easier exports of palm oil, textiles and footwear, and it arrives just before its current EU trade preferences end on the 1st of January. To discuss the deal Airlangga Hartarto, Coordinating Minister for Economic Affairs in Indonesia joined us on the programme. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea9d9328-e5f6-11f1-b921-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a5f225a1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea9d9328-e5f6-11f1-b921-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a5f225a1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">42bad4e0-e5f6-11f1-b300-9d257b5e701b</guid><pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 22:23:03 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Truth or Fake</category><title>Was Vladimir Putin 'snubbed' by Xi Jinping? Viral handshake video is AI fake</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261121-was-vladimir-putin-snubbed-by-xi-jinping-viral-handshake-video-is-ai-fake</link><description>
A viral video appearing to show China's Xi Jinping "snubbing" Vladimir Putin at the APEC summit has racked up millions of views, fuelling speculation about tensions between Beijing and Moscow. But the footage is fake. FRANCE 24 traced the original video to an earlier summit, where the two leaders did shake hands. The creator, who runs a satire account, admitted to manipulating the footage using Grok Imagine, an AI tool developed by Elon Musk's xAI, as Vedika Bahl explains. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1b200bec-e594-11f1-aa9a-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-7ab1fae9.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1b200bec-e594-11f1-aa9a-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-7ab1fae9.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">701c5ae4-e58d-11f1-81f9-fb033d4bd19e</guid><pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 10:16:44 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Vedika BAHL</dc:creator></item><item><category>The World This Week</category><title>'Friends', but who benefits? Myanmar's genocide ruling, Time to 'bre-join' the EU?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-world-this-week/20261120-friends-but-who-benefits-notes-from-an-historic-summit-myanmar-s-genocide-ruling-time-to-bre-join-the-eu</link><description>
This week, even the most superlative headline writing couldn't quite capture the moment and unprecedented nature of US President Donald Trump's three-way summit with Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin in China. We assess the choreography and the consequences. The past seven days also saw another historic moment as Myanmar became the first ever state found to have committed genocide in a ruling by the ICJ. Plus it's been a week that saw Britain "reset" its relations with the EU. What happens next?
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cfefa95a-e518-11f1-b6ef-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-19T230006Z-1256675834-RC2WQOACA4BG-RTRMADP-3-CYBERSECURITY-DRONES-RISK.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cfefa95a-e518-11f1-b6ef-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-19T230006Z-1256675834-RC2WQOACA4BG-RTRMADP-3-CYBERSECURITY-DRONES-RISK.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6a8d1624-e513-11f1-bcb6-1d361a2029ae</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:42:58 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Gavin LEE</dc:creator></item><item><category>Access Asia</category><title>Shenzhen trilateral: Xi Jinping's 'masterclass in diplomatic theatre'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/access-asia/20261120-shenzhen-trilateral-xi-jinping-s-masterclass-in-diplomatic-theatre</link><description>
Xi Jinping positioned himself as a great statesman when he met with the US president and Russia's Vladimir Putin for trilateral talks in Shenzhen this week. But while the Chinese president sparkled on the world stage, does he have anything to show for it? Seong-Hyon Lee, an associate at Harvard University Asia Center, tells us how the trilateral has come up short on deliverables.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2bb9420a-e4f1-11f1-aeb6-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/TRUMP-JINPING-POUTINE-000-D7XR8CA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2bb9420a-e4f1-11f1-aeb6-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/TRUMP-JINPING-POUTINE-000-D7XR8CA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">157a2bc8-e4ef-11f1-a363-9125c0ae01ff</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:37:05 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Delano D’SOUZA</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>Malaysia's unwanted refugees: The plight of the Rohingya</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261120-malaysia-s-unwanted-refugees-the-plight-of-the-rohingya</link><description>
On November 19, the International Court of Justice delivered a historic ruling: finding Myanmar guilty of committing genocide against the Rohingya in 2017. Malaysia has long been a supporter of the persecuted Muslim minority. But its role as a safe haven is coming to an end, amid a rise in hate speech and calls for the expulsion of Rohingya via social media. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a4133a2c-e441-11f1-beda-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-NW-OOV-ECRAN-FREEZE-MALAYSIA-ROHIGNYA-RACISM-BAIN-Lauren.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a4133a2c-e441-11f1-beda-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-NW-OOV-ECRAN-FREEZE-MALAYSIA-ROHIGNYA-RACISM-BAIN-Lauren.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1a5ec6f0-e443-11f1-91a8-93c014f0cee0</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 11:28:30 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Théo Collet</dc:creator></item><item><category>Asia / Pacific</category><title>Was the historic Shenzhen summit a political win or humiliation for Putin?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20261120-was-the-historic-shenzhen-summit-political-win-or-humiliation-for-putin</link><description>
The world watched with bated breath Thursday as the presidents of China, Russia and the United States met for 90 minutes for an unprecedented trilateral summit in Shenzhen. Each leader came to the negotiating table with their own motivations. But Russia's Vladimir Putin was seen as walking away worse off than his counterparts, with some media outlets reporting the Kremlin leader was "humiliated" during the talks. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b2f34066-e421-11f1-999e-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/main-shenzen-poutine.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b2f34066-e421-11f1-999e-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/main-shenzen-poutine.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e2ed850e-e4be-11f1-86b8-9304150b5931</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 10:42:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b2f34066-e421-11f1-999e-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/main-shenzen-poutine.png">© Studio graphique France Médias Monde</source><dc:creator>Sébastian SEIBT</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Xi-Trump-Putin trilateral meeting: Trump's recognition of China and Russia as 'peers of the US'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/xi-trump-putin-trilateral-meeting-trump-s-recognition-of-china-and-russia-as-peers-of-the-us</link><description>
Genie Godula is pleased to welcome Olivia Cheung. The trilateral meeting between Donald Trump, Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin at the APEC summit was significant despite the absence of concrete agreements, according to Cheung. “The significance is seen in the precedence of the United States seeing and giving recognition that China and Russia are peers of the United States,” she explained, adding that “the image of a G3 is very significant.”
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/61a7631a-e447-11f1-ad61-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-130423-130957-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/61a7631a-e447-11f1-ad61-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-130423-130957-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6767ccea-e447-11f1-b2e9-bbd2514180c2</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 19:33:56 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Asia / Pacific</category><title>What impact will the Shenzhen summit have on the US-China trade war?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20261119-what-impact-will-shenzhen-summit-us-china-trade-war</link><description>
Despite warm words between US President Donald Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping at three-way talks with Russian President Vladimir Putin in Shenzhen Thursday, the simmering trade war between Washington and Beijing still looks to be no closer to resolution.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e19cd550-e42e-11f1-8850-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-19T103254Z-1184575046-RC2XQOAUVE6M-RTRMADP-3-APEC-SUMMIT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e19cd550-e42e-11f1-8850-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-19T103254Z-1184575046-RC2XQOAUVE6M-RTRMADP-3-APEC-SUMMIT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">78f1a7a6-e42e-11f1-90b4-3bc47462f08d</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 17:34:09 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e19cd550-e42e-11f1-8850-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-19T103254Z-1184575046-RC2XQOAUVE6M-RTRMADP-3-APEC-SUMMIT.jpg">© Vyacheslav Prokofyev, Sputnik via Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Bangladesh: Hasina supporters vandalise ruling party office</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261119-bangladesh-hasina-supporters-vandalise-ruling-party-office</link><description>
Hundreds of supporters of ousted Bangladesh premier Sheikh Hasina staged street protests on Thursday, as a wave of arson attacks escalated tensions ahead of her planned return from exile next month. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5276727a-e43b-11f1-a52f-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/Bangladesh-protests-Hasina0.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5276727a-e43b-11f1-a52f-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/Bangladesh-protests-Hasina0.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">86c1ef50-e43b-11f1-a5c4-cbf5d8d01bb9</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 17:14:23 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>ICJ ruling on Myanmar genocide: ‘Historic victory for Rohingya, landmark for international justice’</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/icj-ruling-on-myanmar-genocide-historic-victory-for-rohingya-landmark-for-international-justice</link><description>
Erin Ogunkeye welcomes Reed Brody, Member of the International Commission of Jurists, Human Rights Lawyer and veteran war crimes prosecutor. The International Court of Justice's ruling on Myanmar marks, according to Brody, "a historic victory for the Rohingya, but also a landmark for international justice." He explains how the court assessed evidence of genocidal intent, rejecting Myanmar's justification of its military operations as counterterrorism. He argues that "the existence of an armed conflict or a security objective doesn't exclude genocidal intent," a finding he believes will have "far-reaching implications in future genocide cases," including South Africa's case against Israel over Gaza. Brody also discusses the Gambia's role in bringing the case and growing challenges to international accountability, warning that "we're in a very dangerous moment."
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/96bb2d4a-e42c-11f1-8790-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-150421-151554-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/96bb2d4a-e42c-11f1-8790-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-150421-151554-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9c398dac-e42c-11f1-b7c2-8da47a19b1b5</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:54:42 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title><![CDATA[Xi demonstrating China's 'global status', occupying 'central position' in relation to Trump & Putin]]></title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/xi-demonstrating-china-s-global-status-occupying-central-position-in-relation-to-trump-putin</link><description>
Sharon Gaffney is pleased to welcome Rex Li, professor of international relations at King's College London. For Professor Li, the meeting between Xi Jinping, Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin in China is less about "reaching concrete agreements and a common position." Rather, he argues, it is about demonstrating Xi's ability to bring the American and Russian leaders together to discuss major geopolitical issues. Li believes that Xi Jinping has the most to gain from the meeting, as it allows him to demonstrate China's global status and its role as a diplomatic convener. Putin may also benefit from the opportunity to meet the two leaders despite Russia's international isolation following its invasion of Ukraine.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2b545312-e425-11f1-858e-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-223636-224406-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2b545312-e425-11f1-858e-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-223636-224406-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">309fed5e-e425-11f1-8223-d3cdd431ccfa</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:40:14 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>ICJ rules Myanmar committed genocide against the Rohingya</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/icj-rules-myanmar-committed-genocide-against-the-rohingya</link><description>
In August 2017, after attacks by a Rohingya militia, Myanmar’s army launched a “clearance campaign” in Rakhine state, with survivors describing mass killings, rapes and burned villages as hundreds of thousands fled to Bangladesh. The Gambia took Myanmar to the ICJ in 2019, and after a seven-year legal fight the court has ruled that Myanmar committed acts of genocide. Meanwhile some 1.2 million Rohingya remain in crowded camps, unable to return home.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1839856c-e421-11f1-9169-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-140816-141029-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1839856c-e421-11f1-9169-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-140816-141029-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">20e93dec-e421-11f1-92ab-575711e6d5a3</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:07:11 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>China summit ends with little new, Europe backs Ukraine in Berlin</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/china-summit-ends-with-little-new-europe-backs-ukraine-in-berlin</link><description>
The leaders of China, the United States and Russia met in Shenzhen for a historic first, but the summit brought little new: non-binding statements on peace and no plan to meet regularly. In Berlin, Europeans gathered to show their support for Ukraine, a pointed answer to a summit where Europe had no seat, as FRANCE 24's Kethevane Gorjestani explains.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/edf9490a-e41f-11f1-8a6b-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-140128-140711-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/edf9490a-e41f-11f1-8a6b-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-140128-140711-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f3513e08-e41f-11f1-ae06-0db31b1ab018</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:04:03 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>In South Korea, women forced into prostitution on US military bases seek justice</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261119-in-south-korea-women-forced-into-prostitution-on-us-military-bases-seek-justice</link><description>
Tens of thousands of women were forced to work in South Korean state-sanctioned brothels used by American soldiers based in the country after the Korean War. They were raped, physically abused and forcibly confined. Now, after decades of silence, some of these women are speaking out.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea501f5a-e42d-11f1-8d9d-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-NW-PKG-F24-FOCUS-S-KOREA-COMFORT-WOMEN-NO-MIX-Sub-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ea501f5a-e42d-11f1-8d9d-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-NW-PKG-F24-FOCUS-S-KOREA-COMFORT-WOMEN-NO-MIX-Sub-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e70e7ca2-e378-11f1-a64e-2b8c1462b591</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 13:21:41 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Chloé BORGNON</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'Their lives, their rights, their history': Not the end of accountability for the Rohingya people'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/their-lives-their-rights-their-history-not-the-end-of-accountability-for-the-rohingya-people</link><description>
Oliver Farry welcomes Sonya Sceats, Director and CEO of the British Institute of International and Comparative Law. The case brought by The Gambia against Myanmar before the ICJ, and the court ruling, both have great significance for the Rohingya people, the interpretation of the Genocide Convention and the possibility of future criminal proceedings. The case could also have important implications for South Africa's proceedings against Israel, where there are “many similarities between the two cases.”
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/51e634f6-e415-11f1-a6b4-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-081406-081457-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/51e634f6-e415-11f1-a6b4-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-081406-081457-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">572743f6-e415-11f1-a6c1-633ba3d721f8</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 13:19:21 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Asia / Pacific</category><title>What to know about Xi, Putin and Trump's historic trilateral summit</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20261119-what-to-know-about-xi-putin-and-trump-s-historic-trilateral-summit</link><description>
China's Xi Jinping, Russian President Vladimir Putin and his US counterpart Donald Trump met for a historic summit in Shenzhen, China, on Thursday, where they agreed on a "peace declaration". The three leaders pledged to reform the UN, increase supplies of refined petroleum products and uphold the immunity enjoyed by officials in international courts.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1625fff6-e35b-11f1-9206-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-18T132731Z-1890202118-RC2BQOA4C91Q-RTRMADP-3-APEC-SUMMIT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1625fff6-e35b-11f1-9206-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-18T132731Z-1890202118-RC2BQOA4C91Q-RTRMADP-3-APEC-SUMMIT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f2939d4c-e417-11f1-8b29-e39107973080</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 13:11:27 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1625fff6-e35b-11f1-9206-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-18T132731Z-1890202118-RC2BQOA4C91Q-RTRMADP-3-APEC-SUMMIT.jpg">© Iori Sagisawa, Reuters </source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Myanmar's Rohingya case: how does the ICJ define genocide?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/myanmar-s-rohingya-case-how-does-the-icj-define-genocide</link><description>
The ICJ is preparing to rule on whether Myanmar committed genocide against the Rohingya, in a case brought by The Gambia after the 2017 crackdown. The key legal test is proving intent to destroy a group, which is notoriously hard, and the court’s reading of it could also weigh on South Africa’s case against Israel.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/92ec7f8c-e406-11f1-8385-6d75ed565170/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-111234-111441-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/92ec7f8c-e406-11f1-8385-6d75ed565170/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-111234-111441-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9827df14-e406-11f1-9c04-8da7495eed5e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:22:31 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'We are making history,' says Trump at historic trilateral with Xi and Putin</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261119-we-are-making-history-says-trump-at-historic-trilateral-with-xi-and-putin</link><description>
The first meeting in history between US, Chinese and Russian leaders has kicked off in Shenzhen, China. "We are really making history," said the US president to his counterparts.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4411e162-e402-11f1-9df0-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/Trump-in-Shenzhen-10.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4411e162-e402-11f1-9df0-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/Trump-in-Shenzhen-10.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6f4caf06-e402-11f1-b261-89987c50f799</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:02:02 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>APEC Summit: Lots of gloss, little real progress?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/apec-summit-lots-of-gloss-little-real-progress</link><description>
Xi Jinping, Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin are holding their first-ever trilateral meeting in Shenzhen, on the sidelines of the APEC forum. It is a historic moment in more ways than one, with the leaders of the world’s three most powerful countries around the same table. Ukraine, the war in Iran, nuclear disarmament and artificial intelligence are all expected to be discussed, and the summit has stoked fears of a carve-up of the world into spheres of influence. But despite the buzz, analysts expect more symbolism than substance, with few concrete breakthroughs likely. Our correspondent in the region has more.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/997c2f9a-e3fd-11f1-ad0f-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-100153-100636-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/997c2f9a-e3fd-11f1-ad0f-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-100153-100636-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9eaaabea-e3fd-11f1-845a-456f10c6396e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:47:03 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Xi, Trump, Putin: historic summit ends, outcome unclear</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/xi-trump-putin-historic-summit-ends-outcome-unclear</link><description>
The unprecedented trilateral summit between Xi Jinping, Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin ended on Thursday in Shenzhen after about an hour and 20 minutes. No details have emerged yet, but the three leaders talking face to face is already an event. Each has their own agenda: Putin wants to be back on the world stage, Trump needs a win at home, and Xi is asserting China’s status as an economic superpower. What comes out of it remains unclear, as FRANCE 24's journalists explain.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7fdd47ee-e403-11f1-b15f-7908c4e1a3c7/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-110128-110945-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7fdd47ee-e403-11f1-b15f-7908c4e1a3c7/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-110128-110945-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">84bc56ec-e403-11f1-9a27-6d8abff1f19e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:42:44 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>APEC Summit: Trump, Putin, Xi hold 'historic' meeting</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261119-apec-summit-trump-putin-xi-hold-historic-meeting</link><description>
🇺🇸 🇨🇳 🇷🇺 US President Donald #Trump, Russia's Vladimir #Putin and Chinese leader #Xi Jinping kicked off their first trilateral talks in Shenzhen. 
 
Beijing said the meeting is aimed at "safeguarding world peace", but it has raised fears of a carve up of the globe into "spheres of influence".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5f96f0b2-e3fd-11f1-9395-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/mini-apec.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5f96f0b2-e3fd-11f1-9395-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/mini-apec.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7d0c4156-e3fd-11f1-860e-0d17f72cf4df</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:19:42 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Xi Jinping, Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin begin unprecedented summit in China</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/xi-jinping-donald-trump-and-vladimir-putin-begin-unprecedented-summit-in-china</link><description>
Xi Jinping, Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin opened their first trilateral talks in Shenzhen on the sidelines of the APEC forum, a meeting analysts expect to offer more symbolism than breakthroughs and that critics fear could carve the world into spheres of influence. Ukraine, the Iran war, nuclear disarmament and AI are all expected to come up, while Trump and Xi also held one-on-one talks on trade and technology.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5f550b2c-e3f6-11f1-aad1-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-093449-094417-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5f550b2c-e3f6-11f1-aad1-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-093449-094417-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">647ecffc-e3f6-11f1-b746-8fe6181820c5</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 09:07:17 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>South Korea tightens security for national exam over AI cheating scandal</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/south-korea-tightens-security-for-national-exam-over-ai-cheating-scandal</link><description>
More than 550,000 candidates sat South Korea’s Suneung university entrance exam under extraordinary security, after an AI cheating scandal in which a top scorer on a national tax accountant exam admitted using a hidden mini camera to send questions to an AI system. Authorities inspected students’ glasses, deployed thousands of extra supervisors and ordered stickers over every button on candidates’ clothing. A nationwide disaster alert was also sent to make even muted phones buzz, exposing any devices smuggled into exam rooms.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3847c0a2-e3f5-11f1-ba0e-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-091253-091442-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3847c0a2-e3f5-11f1-ba0e-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-091253-091442-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3ce4ab02-e3f5-11f1-9a67-a5616fcc811e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 08:55:02 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Nuclear arms at the center of the Shenzhen trilateral meeting</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/nuclear-arms-at-the-center-of-the-schenzhen-trilateral-meeting</link><description>
Nuclear weapons are at the top of Donald Trump’s agenda at the trilateral summit in Beijing. The US president wants the United States, Russia and China to reduce their nuclear stockpiles, and FRANCE 24’s Philip Turle expects him to push hard for a three-way agreement on the issue. Iran is also a key part of the discussion. The three leaders share an interest in preventing the Iranian regime from acquiring nuclear weapons. Regional conflicts and oil prices are also se to be central issues.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ca689c6e-e3ef-11f1-82ff-1ba7ddfab527/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-080626-081405-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ca689c6e-e3ef-11f1-82ff-1ba7ddfab527/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261119-080626-081405-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">cfea8e2c-e3ef-11f1-b22d-87f5f28dde80</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 08:36:29 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
